# Custom Directives

A Plugin can own Directive types. A handler returns one as data, and the Plugin reduces it into its state, dispatches it after commit, or does both. In this tutorial you build one Directive of each kind. Run the cells in order.

## Install Jido

```elixir
Mix.install([
  {:jido, github: "mikehostetler/new_jido", branch: "main"}
])
```

## Define a Directive

A custom Directive is a struct whose module uses `Jido.Agent.Directive`. That macro adds a default `validate/1`, which applies `schema/0` when the module defines one.

```elixir
defmodule PluginDirectiveGuide.AddLabel do
  use Jido.Agent.Directive

  @schema Jido.Joi.struct(__MODULE__, %{name: Jido.Joi.string() |> Jido.Joi.min(1)}, coerce: true)
  @enforce_keys Jido.Joi.enforce_keys(@schema)
  defstruct Jido.Joi.struct_fields(@schema)

  def schema, do: @schema
end

{:ok, validated} = PluginDirectiveGuide.AddLabel.validate(struct!(PluginDirectiveGuide.AddLabel, name: "billing"))
validated.name
```

The cell returns `"billing"`.

## Reduce a Directive into state

The handler requests the change, and the Plugin applies it. `directives/1` claims the type. `reduce/2` finds its Directives in `reduction.directives`, which holds the whole batch, so filter by struct.

```elixir
defmodule PluginDirectiveGuide.Labels do
  use Jido.Plugin

  alias PluginDirectiveGuide.AddLabel

  @impl true
  def state_spec(_opts), do: {:labels, Jido.Joi.list(Jido.Joi.string()) |> Jido.Joi.default([])}

  @impl true
  def directives(_opts), do: [AddLabel]

  @impl true
  def reduce(reduction, _opts) do
    added = for %AddLabel{name: name} <- reduction.directives, do: name
    {:ok, Enum.uniq(reduction.plugin_state ++ added)}
  end
end

defmodule PluginDirectiveGuide.Ticket do
  use Jido.Agent, name: "ticket", plugins: [PluginDirectiveGuide.Labels]

  state do
    schema Jido.Joi.object(%{open: Jido.Joi.boolean() |> Jido.Joi.default(true)})
  end

  routes do
    signal_source "/support/ticket"

    route "ticket.label", as: :label do
      action %{name: name},
        schema: Jido.Joi.object(%{name: Jido.Joi.string()}),
        context: context do
        {:ok, context.agent_state, [%PluginDirectiveGuide.AddLabel{name: name}]}
      end
    end
  end
end

ticket = PluginDirectiveGuide.Ticket.instantiate!(id: "t-1")

{:ok, labeled, [%PluginDirectiveGuide.AddLabel{name: "billing"}]} =
  PluginDirectiveGuide.Ticket.cmd(ticket, PluginDirectiveGuide.Ticket.label_signal!(%{name: "billing"}))

labeled.state
```

The cell returns `%{open: true, labels: ["billing"]}`. The pure path still returns the batch, so a caller or a test can inspect it.

## See validation fail

Jido validates every Directive before commit. One invalid Directive fails the whole Turn with no state change. Here an empty name breaks the schema's `min(1)` rule.

```elixir
{:error, [issue]} =
  PluginDirectiveGuide.Ticket.cmd(ticket, PluginDirectiveGuide.Ticket.label_signal!(%{name: ""}))

issue.code
```

The cell returns `:greater_than_or_equal_to`. The error is the reason that `validate/1` returned, here the schema's list of issues.

A Directive with no owner on this Agent fails the same way. This Agent returns `AddLabel` but does not declare the `Labels` Plugin.

```elixir
defmodule PluginDirectiveGuide.NoOwner do
  use Jido.Agent, name: "no_owner"

  routes do
    signal_source "/support/no_owner"

    route "ticket.label", as: :label do
      action _input, schema: Jido.Joi.object(%{}), context: context do
        {:ok, context.agent_state, [%PluginDirectiveGuide.AddLabel{name: "billing"}]}
      end
    end
  end
end

{:error, unowned} =
  PluginDirectiveGuide.NoOwner.cmd(
    PluginDirectiveGuide.NoOwner.instantiate!(id: "t-2"),
    PluginDirectiveGuide.NoOwner.label_signal!(%{})
  )

unowned.message
```

The cell returns `"Agent Directive has no owner"`.

## Dispatch a Directive after commit

A Directive that requests work outside the Agent uses `dispatch/4`. It runs only in `Jido.AgentServer`, after the commit, in its own task. The arguments are the Plugin's runtime process or `nil`, the Directive, a `Jido.Plugin.Context` with `phase: :directive`, and the Plugin's options.

The next cell starts a small mailbox process that stands in for an email service. The `:mailbox` option names it.

```elixir
{:ok, _mailbox} = Agent.start(fn -> [] end, name: PluginDirectiveGuide.Mailbox)

defmodule PluginDirectiveGuide.SendEmail do
  use Jido.Agent.Directive

  @schema Jido.Joi.struct(
            __MODULE__,
            %{
              to: Jido.Joi.string() |> Jido.Joi.min(1),
              subject: Jido.Joi.string(),
              fail: Jido.Joi.boolean() |> Jido.Joi.default(false)
            },
            coerce: true
          )
  @enforce_keys Jido.Joi.enforce_keys(@schema)
  defstruct Jido.Joi.struct_fields(@schema)

  def schema, do: @schema
end

defmodule PluginDirectiveGuide.Email do
  use Jido.Plugin, option_keys: [agent_server: [:mailbox]]

  alias PluginDirectiveGuide.SendEmail
  alias Jido.Plugin.Context

  @impl true
  def directives(_opts), do: [SendEmail]

  @impl true
  def dispatch(_runtime, %SendEmail{fail: true}, %Context{}, _opts), do: {:error, :mail_service_down}

  def dispatch(_runtime, %SendEmail{} = email, %Context{phase: :directive} = context, opts) do
    message = %{to: email.to, subject: email.subject, state_version: context.state_version}
    Agent.update(opts[:mailbox], &[message | &1])
  end
end

defmodule PluginDirectiveGuide.Notifier do
  use Jido.Agent,
    name: "notifier",
    plugins: [{PluginDirectiveGuide.Email, %{mailbox: PluginDirectiveGuide.Mailbox}}]

  state do
    schema Jido.Joi.object(%{closed: Jido.Joi.integer() |> Jido.Joi.default(0)})
  end

  routes do
    signal_source "/support/notifier"

    route "ticket.close", as: :close do
      action %{to: to, fail: fail},
        schema: Jido.Joi.object(%{to: Jido.Joi.string(), fail: Jido.Joi.boolean() |> Jido.Joi.default(false)}),
        context: context do
        emails = [
          %PluginDirectiveGuide.SendEmail{to: to, subject: "Your ticket closed", fail: fail},
          %PluginDirectiveGuide.SendEmail{to: "audit@example.com", subject: "Ticket closed"}
        ]

        {:ok, %{context.agent_state | closed: context.agent_state.closed + 1}, emails}
      end
    end
  end
end

pure = PluginDirectiveGuide.Notifier.instantiate!(id: "n-pure")

{:ok, %{state: %{closed: 1}}, [_customer, _audit]} =
  PluginDirectiveGuide.Notifier.cmd(pure, PluginDirectiveGuide.Notifier.close_signal!(%{to: "ada@example.com"}))

Agent.get(PluginDirectiveGuide.Mailbox, & &1)
```

The cell returns `[]`. The pure path returned the Directives but ran nothing.

Now run the same Turn live. `Jido.AgentServer.call/3` replies at commit, before post-commit work finishes, so this cell waits for the server to return to `:idle`.

```elixir
{:ok, _instance} = Jido.start(name: PluginDirectiveGuide.Jido)
{:ok, server} = Jido.start_agent(PluginDirectiveGuide.Jido, PluginDirectiveGuide.Notifier, id: "n-live")
:ok = Jido.AgentServer.await_ready(server)

wait_for_idle = fn ->
  Enum.find(1..200, fn _attempt ->
    Process.sleep(10)
    Jido.AgentServer.status(server).phase == :idle
  end)
end

{:ok, %{state: %{closed: 1}}, [_, _]} =
  Jido.AgentServer.call(server, PluginDirectiveGuide.Notifier.close_signal!(%{to: "ada@example.com"}))

wait_for_idle.()

PluginDirectiveGuide.Mailbox |> Agent.get(& &1) |> Enum.reverse() |> Enum.map(&{&1.to, &1.state_version})
```

The cell returns `[{"ada@example.com", 1}, {"audit@example.com", 1}]`. The two emails went out in batch order, and both saw the committed revision.

## Handle a dispatch failure

A failed dispatch stops the rest of the batch and does not undo the commit. The caller has already received its result. Look at `last_effect_outcome` in `Jido.AgentServer.status/1` to see what happened.

```elixir
Agent.update(PluginDirectiveGuide.Mailbox, fn _ -> [] end)

{:ok, %{state: %{closed: 2}}, [_, _]} =
  Jido.AgentServer.call(server, PluginDirectiveGuide.Notifier.close_signal!(%{to: "ada@example.com", fail: true}))

wait_for_idle.()

status = Jido.AgentServer.status(server)
outcome = status.last_effect_outcome
sent = Agent.get(PluginDirectiveGuide.Mailbox, & &1)
:ok = Jido.stop_agent(PluginDirectiveGuide.Jido, server)

{status.last_outcome.status, outcome.status, outcome.completed, outcome.total, sent}
```

The cell returns `{:succeeded, :failed, 0, 2, []}`. The Turn succeeded. Its effects failed on the first Directive, and the audit email never ran. Jido does not retry a dispatch, so make it safe to skip, or have a later Turn repair the result.

## Dispatch context

`dispatch/4` receives a `Jido.Plugin.Context` with these fields:

| Field | Value |
|---|---|
| `:phase` | `:directive` |
| `:turn_id`, `:agent_id` | Identity of this Turn and Agent. |
| `:source_signal` | The Signal that the server received. |
| `:effective_signal` | The Signal that the Turn used. |
| `:state_version` | The committed revision. |
| `:plugin_state` | The committed value of this Plugin's field, or `nil`. |
| `:turn_context` | The transient context the caller passed. |
| `:jido`, `:partition` | The instance and partition, or `nil`. |

The batch shares the `directive_timeout` budget, 5,000 milliseconds by default. Running past it stops the batch with status `:timed_out`. A dispatch cannot make a synchronous request to its own AgentServer. That request returns `{:error, :reentrant_turn}`. Use `Jido.AgentServer.cast/2` to send a follow-up Signal.

## Rules

- A Directive module must define a struct and `validate/1`. A custom `validate/1` returns `{:ok, directive}` of the same type or `{:error, reason}`.
- A Directive type has one owner, and a Plugin cannot own a built-in Directive.
- An owned Directive needs `reduce/2` or `dispatch/4` on its Plugin. `dispatch/4` requires `directives/1`.
- A Turn cannot add, configure, or remove a Plugin and also return that Plugin's Directives.

